From 40e219ac8bc05a349950766408daea74c77c57bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JuggyMcNutty <11588877+JuggyMcNutty@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:21:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] VM: conditions and operands as plain values

FORK ONLY. This project ships a NO-AI Code Rule asking that LLM-written
changes stay in a fork and are never PR'd upstream. These were written with
Claude. Do not submit them.

On Liberty Island, 45% of the statements the VM runs are conditions
(JumpIfNot), and a minute's desktop run evaluates the fast operators 31
million times, their operands mostly variables, constants, int-to-float
conversions and other operators. Each of those values went into an 88-byte
ExpressionValue -- constructed, moved up by assignment through a type
switch, converted, destructed -- to give one bool, number or object.

Each expression node is now classified when first met
(Expression::TypedKind): a local or instance variable of a plain type, a
constant, a conversion, && or ||, Skip, or a fast operator whose operands'
types its conversions accept. The typed evaluators (EvalBool, EvalNumber,
EvalObject, EvalName) read and compute those directly; any other node
(Generic) goes through Value and ToBool, ToInt and the rest as before. They
serve JumpIfNot's condition, && and ||, the fast operators wherever they
are called, and statements assigning to a plain local or instance variable
(Let, LetBool), stored as ExpressionValue::Store stores.

On the handheld the cost of these nodes is mostly waiting on memory, so a
typed node carries what it reads: a variable node (and a BoolVariable) its
variable's offset and bool mask, an operator its operands -- not a
UProperty, an inner node or an argument array away. The general path's
fallback is out of line, so the typed evaluators keep no ExpressionValue
of their own.

Each gives what the general path gave, with the same C++ conversions:

- A fast operator is typed only when its operands' types cannot make its
  conversions throw, so nothing escapes the general path's catch.
- The general path reads operands only once all of them are evaluated: a
  variable operand is read after the operand after it, which might change
  it.
- A None context still goes to Frame::Call, which reports it.

Checked on Liberty Island on the desktop. A temporary check recomputed 1
in 7 typed evaluations of side-effect-free trees through the general path
-- 40 million, every kind that occurs, ++ += and -= with their variable
restored in between -- all identical to the bit. With the frame time and
random seeds temporarily fixed, a hash of every actor's location,
rotation, velocity, state and class matched the build before this one for
4,300 frames: every frame that build itself repeats identically.
